---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/library-science/foundational/foundations-of-library-science">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a foundational lesson in Library Science. You need nothing from earlier lessons —
    no cataloguing, no computing, no prior study of information — only your experience of
    looking for something and not finding it. Everything is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what the discipline actually studies — organised access to information — and why it is a moral enterprise and not merely a technical one;</li>
    <li>trace the library's history from Alexandria to the free public library movement, and name the values that came out of it;</li>
    <li>walk the information life cycle and see it in any library's org chart;</li>
    <li>explain how classification and cataloguing make millions of items findable;</li>
    <li>tell the four library families apart — and say what unites them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Library and information science is the discipline of organised access to knowledge. The visible
    objects of the field are buildings and books, but the actual subject matter is information
    itself: how it is created, how it is described so it can be found again, how it is preserved
    against decay and obsolescence, and — the question that makes the field a moral enterprise and
    not merely a technical one — who gets to reach it. A search engine indexes pages for people
    clicking; a library organises the record of knowledge for people who need it to be
    <em>trustworthy</em>, <em>durable</em> and <em>free at the point of use</em>. Those three
    adjectives are the whole difference, and they explain nearly everything a library does.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider what the trustworthiness claim means in practice. When you search the open web, you get
    answers ranked by advertising economics and engagement signals; when you ask a reference
    librarian, you get sources that have been selected by a professional, catalogued against
    standards that have been refined for 150 years, and kept in circulation by an institution whose
    measure of success is not your time on page but your ability to find what you actually needed.
    Librarianship is, in this sense, the quality-control layer of the information society —
    invisible while it works, and desperately missed when it is absent. Anyone who has tried to
    find a government statistic that has been quietly moved, or a journal article locked behind a
    paywall, has felt the cost of its absence.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "everything is on the internet now", so libraries are warehouses for what a search box already delivers. Most of the recorded past is not digitised — archives, the deep back-runs of journals, government documents, most books out of commercial print — and what is online arrives ranked by advertising economics rather than selected and catalogued against standards. The library's scarce goods, trustworthiness, durability and free access at the point of use, are precisely the ones the open web does not supply, which is why the digitisation era expanded the profession rather than retiring it.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="find the line the everything-is-online myth would love" widget="spot-error" config={flsOnlineSpot} />
  <p>
    This page covers the field's foundations in three passes: the history and values that shaped
    the institution, the information life cycle that explains the machinery, and the family of
    library types that apply the same principles to very different communities. A closing section
    looks at where the discipline is heading — and why its core problem has not changed since
    antiquity.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mission, the pipeline, the values" widget="equationStep" config={foundationsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the order of the steps — mission before machinery, values
    before institutions. The pattern to look for: every section of this page expands one of
    these five steps, and the hardest cases (censorship, privacy, licensing) are decided by
    step 3, the values.
  </p>

  <h2>History and values: from Alexandria to the public library movement</h2>
  <p>
    Libraries are among humanity's oldest institutions. The Library of Alexandria, founded in the
    third century BCE, pursued an ambition any modern library would recognise: to collect a copy of
    every work in the known world, in Greek and in translation, and to organise it for scholarship —
    its scholars produced the first systematic bibliographies and even divided Homer's epics into
    the standard books we still use. Medieval monasteries copied and preserved manuscripts through
    centuries when literacy itself was rare, and the first university libraries grew alongside
    European universities from the twelfth century. But for most of that long history, libraries
    served the few: kings, clergy, scholars who could read Latin.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="scrub the institution's long arc" widget="timeline" config={flsHistoryTimeline} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Library of Alexandria was destroyed in a single cataclysmic fire. The record shows a long decline: Caesar's fire of 48 BCE damaged part of the collection (it was stored near the harbour), later centuries of reduced funding and political instability shrank it further, and it had likely faded from function by late antiquity — no single villain, no dramatic ending. The famous caliph story is a much later legend. The lesson for the field is quieter and sharper: collections die of neglect, not catastrophe — which is why preservation is a budget line, not an act of heroism.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet on how Alexandria actually ended" widget="predict" config={flsAlexandriaPredict} />
  <p>
    The transformation into a democratic institution is essentially a nineteenth-century story. The
    free public library — funded by taxation, open to every resident, no purchase or membership
    required — spread through Britain and America on an argument that was radical for its time:
    that access to knowledge is a public good, like clean water, and should not depend on wealth.
    Andrew Carnegie alone funded more than 2,500 library buildings worldwide, roughly 1,700 of them
    in the United States, on the principle that a man who dies rich dies disgraced — and that the
    poor deserve exactly the same access to books as the rich. In the same era, 1876, two
    foundation stones of the profession were laid: Melvil Dewey published his decimal
    classification, and the American Library Association was founded.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the Carnegie building count" widget="estimate" config={flsCarnegieEstimate} />
  <p>
    Those two events map the field's twin halves: <em>method</em> and <em>values</em>. The values
    were eventually codified. The ALA's Library Bill of Rights (first adopted in 1939) asserts that
    materials should be provided for the interest, information and enlightenment of all people, that
    libraries should challenge censorship, and that no one should be denied service because of
    origin, age, background or views. Intellectual freedom and privacy sit at the centre: the
    profession's position is that what you read is nobody's business — not your neighbours', not
    your employer's, and not the government's — which is why librarians have repeatedly gone to
    court rather than hand over borrowing records, and why many library systems delete circulation
    data as soon as a book is returned. S. R. Ranganathan, the great Indian library scientist,
    compressed the mission into five laws in 1931, and they still read as a manifesto: books are
    for use; every reader his book; every book its reader; save the time of the reader; a library
    is a growing organism.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="line up the five laws as the manifesto reads" widget="order" config={flsLawsOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "neutrality" means the library has no values. The institution is neutral about the reader — anyone may enter, and no reading list is policed — but it is emphatically not neutral about the reader's <em>right</em> to read. Defending a challenged book the librarian personally dislikes is the profession's defining ethical act, and it happens constantly: hundreds of titles are formally challenged in American libraries every year, and the Library Bill of Rights is the framework used to answer those challenges.</p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> for most of their history libraries served the few; the nineteenth century made access to knowledge a public good, and the profession's twin halves — Dewey's method and the ALA's values, both born in 1876 — still define it: neutral about the reader, emphatically not neutral about the reader's right to read.</p>

  <h2>The information life cycle: why the building is shaped the way it is</h2>
  <p>
    Every library function is one station of a pipeline that information travels from creation to
    use. Information is <strong>created</strong> (an author writes, a government publishes, a
    scientist reports); <strong>disseminated</strong> (through publishers, vendors, databases);
    <strong>selected and organised</strong> (acquisitions chooses what the collection needs; cataloguing
    describes it); <strong>preserved</strong> (conservation, digitisation, format migration); and
    finally <strong>used</strong> (circulation lends it, reference staff connect people to it,
    instruction teaches people to evaluate it). Once you see the pipeline, the departmental
    structure of any library stops being mysterious: the org chart is the life cycle.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the pipeline's station names" widget="cloze" config={flsCycleCloze} />
  <p>
    The organisation stage deserves a closer look, because it is the field's core technology. The
    engineering problem is: describe an item so that a person who has never seen it, and did not
    create it, can find it among millions. Dewey's decimal classification solved it by subject:
    ten top-level classes (000 for general works, 500 for science, 800 for literature, and so on),
    subdivided to the decimal place, so that books on the same subject physically stand next to
    each other on the shelf — which is why a book at 510 (mathematics) will always be shelved
    near other books at 510 in any Dewey library on Earth. Academic libraries mostly use the
    Library of Congress classification, which offers finer subject divisions for research
    collections. Alongside classification runs descriptive cataloguing: a standardised record of
    author, title, edition, publisher and subject headings, built to rules that evolved from the
    Anglo-American Cataloguing Rules (AACR2) into RDA (Resource Description and Access, 2010) and
    are increasingly expressed as linked data on the open web. The MARC record format, developed
    by Henriette Avram at the Library of Congress in the 1960s, let these records be exchanged
    between libraries — the reason a catalogue can "borrow" records from a national bibliography
    instead of cataloguing from scratch, and the ancestor of every shared catalogue such as
    WorldCat, which indexes the holdings of thousands of libraries worldwide.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each tool with the job it does" widget="match" config={flsToolsMatch} />
  <p>
    Follow one book through the cycle as a worked example. A historian publishes a monograph in
    March. Review journals (<em>Choice</em>, <em>Library Journal</em>) assess it; a university
    library's subject librarian, weighing reviews against course needs and budget, orders it
    (acquisition). The publisher ships it; on arrival it is barcoded, its MARC record is downloaded
    and adjusted, subject headings are assigned, and it is classified and shelved (organisation).
    Twenty years later the paper is brittle; conservators rebind or box it, and a digitised copy is
    archived (preservation). Throughout, it is borrowed, reserved, taught from and cited (use) —
    and usage statistics feed back into the next round of selection. One book, six professional
    decisions, decades of stewardship.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> every library function is one station of a single pipeline — creation, dissemination, organisation, preservation, use — and organisation is the core technology: describing an item so a stranger can find it among millions. Next: the four families of institutions that run the same pipeline for different communities.</p>

  <h2>Types of libraries: one science, many missions</h2>
  <p>
    The same methods and ethics get applied by four great families of institutions, distinguished
    by whom they serve and how they are funded. <strong>Public libraries</strong> serve everyone in
    a jurisdiction; their collections are broad and responsive to local needs, and their remit has
    quietly expanded into the social safety net — internet access, job-search help, children's
    programming, shelter on a hot afternoon. They are funded by local taxation and governed by
    boards, which makes them unusually accountable to (and unusually vulnerable to) local politics.
    <strong>Academic libraries</strong> serve a university's teaching and research mission: deep
    subject collections, expensive journal subscriptions, liaison librarians embedded in
    departments, and an instructional role teaching students to find and evaluate literature —
    information literacy is now an accredited learning outcome in higher education. <strong>School
    libraries</strong>, run by certified teacher-librarians in K-12 settings, sit exactly at the
    junction of literacy and curiosity, and a large body of research links staffed school libraries
    to reading achievement. <strong>Special libraries</strong> — law firm libraries, hospital
    libraries, corporate research services, government libraries — serve one organisation's precise
    needs; the law library and the hospital's clinical knowledge service are the classic cases.
    Around them stand the great <strong>national libraries</strong> (the Library of Congress, the
    British Library, the Bibliothèque nationale de France) with legal deposit — the right to a copy
    of everything published in the country — plus archives, which preserve the records of
    organisations, and museums, with which libraries increasingly share standards and staff.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file each trait by its library family" widget="sort-buckets" config={flsFamilyBuckets} />
  <p>
    What unites the family is more important than what separates them. Every one of them selects,
    describes, preserves and mediates information under the same ethical framework, uses shared
    standards (you will find Dewey in a school, MARC everywhere, RDA in every cataloguing course),
    and employs professionals trained in the same accredited programmes. A librarian can move from
    a public system to a law library to an academic post because the science — the organisation of
    information and the values of access — is the same; only the community changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The library is "neutral about the reader but not about the reader's right to read" — what does that mean in practice?</li>
    <li>What engineering problem does classification solve, and why does Dewey shelving put books on the same subject side by side?</li>
    <li>Where in the information life cycle do open access, digital preservation and discovery systems each intervene?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Anyone may enter and no reading list is policed, but defending a challenged book the librarian personally dislikes is the profession's defining ethical act, answered through the Library Bill of Rights. (2) Describing an item so a stranger can find it among millions: subject notation gives books on the same subject the same class number, so they physically stand together on the shelf. (3) Open access attacks dissemination, digital preservation attacks preservation, and discovery systems attack organisation — each one stage of the life cycle.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Library science is the discipline of organised access to knowledge: trustworthy, durable and free at the point of use.</li>
    <li>Access to knowledge is a public good — the radical nineteenth-century argument behind the free public library.</li>
    <li>The profession's twin halves are method (Dewey, 1876) and values (the ALA, 1876; the Library Bill of Rights, 1939).</li>
    <li>Every library function is a station of the information life cycle: creation, dissemination, organisation, preservation, use.</li>
    <li>Classification plus descriptive cataloguing solve the core problem: findability among millions.</li>
    <li>Public, academic, school and special libraries differ in community and funding, not in science or ethics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The foundations set here — mission, life cycle, values, families — are the frame the rest
    of the subject hangs on: later lessons take up the stages one at a time, from reference and
    instruction to cataloguing, preservation and the ethical hard cases of the digital era. The
    constant to carry forward is the one Ranganathan stated: the mission is fixed; the
    technologies are the variable.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline's open questions cluster where the internet meets the life cycle. <strong>Open
    access</strong> attacks the dissemination stage: most research is publicly funded yet published
    behind paywalls, and the movement — repositories, open-access mandates, transformative
    agreements — is reshaping academic libraries from licensees back into publishers and curators.
    <strong>Digital preservation</strong> attacks preservation: a medieval manuscript survives
    passively for centuries, but a 1995 CD-ROM or a link-checked web page can die within years, so
    the field's modern problem is that digital information survives only through constant, planned
    action — format migration, checksums, redundant storage, preservation metadata — which is why
    frameworks like the Open Archival Information System reference model exist. <strong>Discovery
    systems and linked data</strong> attack organisation: catalogues are merging into unified
    search layers, and the library's structured records are being released onto the semantic web,
    where Wikidata and library authorities interlink.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hardest questions, though, remain the ethical ones, and they are getting harder. Algorithmic
    recommendation systems in library discovery raise the privacy stakes the profession has always
    guarded. Ebook licensing has handed publishers unprecedented control over public-library
    lending — a library owns a printed book forever but merely licenses an ebook, on terms that
    have seen public libraries pay several times the consumer price per copy with lending caps.
    And the definition of "collection" keeps widening: datasets, software, social media archives and
    oral histories all now demand description and preservation, which is why the field increasingly
    calls itself library and <em>information</em> science. Through every technical shift, Ranganathan's
    five laws have aged remarkably well — because none of them mentions a format. The mission —
    every reader, their book, their information, saved time — is the constant; the technologies are
    the variable.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ALA Core Competences of Librarianship; Rubin &amp; Rubin, <em>Foundations of Library and Information Science</em>; Ranganathan, <em>The Five Laws of Library Science</em> (1931). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
